content.thinking:
The repo's tool is `node scripts/repoint-spec-anchors.mjs [base-ref]` — it repoints anchors from a baseline (origin/main by default). Note the caveat: "A spec whose anchor set (the ordered list of test paths it cites) differs from the base ref is skipped whole: its anchors were authored against the working tree, and no baseline exists to repoint them from."

The spec here cites `onErrorNonError.acceptance.test.ts#L46/L57/L75` — those anchors were authored on this branch (the acceptance file doesn't exist on main), so the spec's anchor set differs from origin/main → the script will SKIP this spec whole. That's what the previous commit meant: "anchors repointed against origin/main (601 up to date, none unresolved)" — it repointed others but skipped specs authored on the branch. So I must fix those anchors BY HAND: recompute the line numbers of tests 2 and 3 after my restructure, and edit the spec's `#L57` and `#L75` (and #L46 if it moves — test 1's line shouldn't move since the helper goes after turnOutcome and before describe... wait, no! If I insert the helper between `turnOutcome` and `describe`, the describe block and all tests shift DOWN by the helper's length. Test 1 at #L46 would move to ~L60ish.

Alternative: put the helper AFTER the describe block (end of file)? Then test lines don't shift. But callee-below-caller doesn't apply to arrows; declare-near-use skips nested refs. A helper at the bottom is used by tests above it — that inverts the newspaper style the repo prefers (caller above callee). Hmm, but the rule only governs FunctionDeclarations. For arrow consts, existing file style: all helpers at top. The style reads: fixtures at top, then the describe. Adding a 5th helper at the bottom would look odd.

Since anchors must be hand-fixed anyway (the spec is branch-authored, skipped by the script), cleanest is: insert the helper in its natural place (after `turnOutcome`), then recompute anchor lines by hand. The anchors must land on the it/test/describe declaration line itself ("An anchor into a test file must additionally land on the it/test/describe declaration itself" — that's the rotten check; well, for require-spec-link the link must fall inside the test's span).

